# Governance ROI: What Does a NERC Violation Actually Cost vs. Unity Catalog?

Your wind utility has 500 turbines across 3 states. NERC CIP
auditors will eventually ask: "Who has access to your CEII data?
Show us the audit trail." If you can't answer, the conversation
gets expensive fast.

This model compares three paths: Unity Catalog (included in
Databricks Premium), doing nothing (and hoping the auditors
don't come), and building your own governance stack from parts.

## The bottom line

| | Annual cost | One-time |
|---|---|---|
| **Unity Catalog governance** | **about $13K** | **about $4K** (training) |
| **DIY governance** (Atlan + custom engineering) | **about $68K** | — |
| **One NERC audit finding** (typical settlement) | — | **about $750K** |
| **Manual compliance** (spreadsheets + email approvals) | **about $42K** | — |
| **UC break-even** | Pays for itself if it prevents **one finding every 57 years** | |

Unity Catalog's ongoing governance cost is about $13K/year —
roughly the cost of a data engineer spending 3 hours a week on
permissions and audit logs. One NERC finding wipes out 57 years
of that spend. The business case writes itself.

**The rest of this document shows how we got here.** Every
assumption is sourced. Change the numbers and rerun.

---

## Part 1: The Cost of Governance (Unity Catalog)

### What you're paying for

Unity Catalog ships with Databricks Premium tier — which is now
the base tier on AWS and GCP (Standard was retired October 2025;
source: [Databricks pricing](https://www.databricks.com/product/pricing)).
There is no separate Unity Catalog SKU. You don't pay extra for
the catalog, lineage tracking, access controls, or audit logging.

The real cost is operational: someone has to manage it.

### Engineer time

A data engineer manages UC permissions, reviews audit logs weekly,
and responds to access requests from analysts and compliance.
$160K/year fully loaded (salary + benefits + taxes) is mid-range
for a US data engineer (BLS + Levels.fyi, $140K–$180K range).
At 2,000 billable hours/year, that's $80/hour:

engineer_hourly = $80

Governance takes 2–4 hours per week for a 15-analyst team: weekly
audit log review (~1 hour), permission change requests (~1 hour),
and ad hoc compliance questions (~1 hour). We use 3 as a midpoint:

governance_hours_weekly = 3
governance_weekly = engineer_hourly * governance_hours_weekly
governance_annual = governance_weekly/week over 1 year

### System tables storage

Unity Catalog writes audit events to `system.access.audit` — every
query, login, permission change, and table access. For a 15-analyst
team running typical workloads, this generates 1–5 GB/month
(source: [Databricks system tables docs](https://docs.databricks.com/en/administration-guide/system-tables/index.html)).
We use 3 GB as a midpoint. S3 Standard pricing in us-east-1:

audit_storage_monthly = 3 GB * $0.023
audit_storage_annual = audit_storage_monthly/month over 1 year

This is a rounding error, but we include it for completeness.

### Training

The data team (3 engineers) needs 1–2 days to learn Unity Catalog
concepts: the three-level namespace, GRANT/REVOKE semantics,
lineage, system tables, and the Catalog Explorer UI. Two days
(16 hours) at the engineer's loaded rate:

training_hours = 16
training_engineers = 3
training_onetime = engineer_hourly * training_hours * training_engineers

This is a one-time cost in year 1.

### Total governance cost

total_governance_yr1 = governance_annual + audit_storage_annual + training_onetime
total_governance_ongoing = governance_annual + audit_storage_annual

Year 1 (with training): total_governance_yr1.
Year 2 onward: total_governance_ongoing.

---

## Part 2: The Cost of NOT Having Governance

### NERC CIP penalties

NERC (North American Electric Reliability Corporation) enforces
CIP (Critical Infrastructure Protection) standards for the US
power grid. The maximum penalty is **$1,540,000 per violation per
day** as of 2025 (source: [NERC Sanction Guidelines, Appendix 4B](https://www.nerc.com/pa/Stand/Resources/Documents/Appendix_4B_of_the_Rules_of_Procedure_Sanction_Guidelines.pdf)).

nerc_max_daily = $1540000

That's the maximum. What actually happens in practice?

### What audits really cost

**Duke Energy** was fined $10M in 2019 for 127 cybersecurity
violations over 3 years (source: [Utility Dive](https://www.utilitydive.com/news/duke-fined-10m-for-cybersecurity-lapses-since-2015/547528/)).
That averages out to:

duke_total = $10M
duke_violations = 127
duke_per_violation = duke_total / duke_violations

About duke_per_violation per violation — and Duke is a massive
utility with resources to negotiate.

**Typical settlements** for a single audit finding (inadequate
access controls, missing audit trails, CEII exposure) land in
the $500K–$1M range. We use the midpoint:

typical_settlement = $750K

### Your wind utility's exposure

A utility with no programmatic access controls on CEII
(Critical Energy Infrastructure Information) — meaning access
is managed by spreadsheets, email approvals, and hope — will
likely draw 3–5 findings in a first serious audit. Missing audit
trails, no role-based access, no lineage proving report integrity,
no evidence of access reviews. We use 4:

likely_findings = 4
audit_risk = typical_settlement * likely_findings

That's audit_risk in potential exposure from a single audit cycle.
This is not additive in the mathematical sense — settlements are
negotiated as a package — but it represents the order of magnitude.

### Manual compliance cost

Without Unity Catalog, someone still has to do governance. They
just do it badly, with spreadsheets. A compliance officer or
senior analyst spends 10+ hours per week maintaining access
request logs, manually reviewing who queried what, preparing
audit evidence, and chasing down "who approved this access?"
questions:

compliance_hours_weekly = 10
manual_compliance_weekly = engineer_hourly * compliance_hours_weekly
manual_compliance_annual = manual_compliance_weekly/week over 1 year

And even after spending manual_compliance_annual/year on manual
compliance, the evidence it produces is incomplete — no lineage,
no automated audit trail, no proof that the spreadsheet wasn't
edited after the fact. An auditor will not be impressed.

---

## Part 3: The DIY Alternative

What if you want governance but don't want Databricks? You can
build a governance stack from open-source and SaaS components.

### Data catalog + lineage

Apache Ranger (free, open-source) handles access control but not
lineage or discoverability. AWS Lake Formation covers S3 access
but not cross-platform governance. For a real catalog with search,
lineage, and compliance features, you need something like Atlan
or Alation — roughly $3K/month for a team this size (source:
vendor pricing pages, 2024–2025 estimates for 15–30 user tiers):

diy_catalog_monthly = $3000
diy_catalog_annual = diy_catalog_monthly * 12

### Integration engineering

Someone has to connect the catalog to your data platform, build
custom audit logging, wire up access policies that actually work
across tools, and maintain the glue code when any component
upgrades. This is roughly 20% of one data engineer's time:

diy_engineering_pct = 20%
diy_engineering_salary = $160K
diy_engineering = diy_engineering_pct * diy_engineering_salary

### Total DIY governance

diy_total = diy_catalog_annual + diy_engineering

That's diy_total per year — about 5x the cost of UC governance —
and you still don't get native lineage through your Spark
pipelines, integrated audit tables you can query with SQL, or
single-pane access management for notebooks, SQL warehouses, and
ML endpoints.

---

## Part 4: The Break-Even Question

Here's where the math gets absurd:

uc_ongoing = total_governance_ongoing
one_finding = typical_settlement
breakeven_years = one_finding / uc_ongoing

Unity Catalog governance costs about uc_ongoing per year. One
NERC finding costs about one_finding. That means UC pays for
itself if it prevents **one finding every breakeven_years years**.

Put differently: if there is a greater than 2% chance of a NERC
finding in any given year, Unity Catalog has positive expected
value purely on regulatory risk. This ignores every other benefit
— lineage for debugging pipelines, discoverability for analysts,
access controls that actually work.

### The real question

The ROI calculation is almost beside the point. The real question
is: **can you afford to tell a NERC auditor that your access
controls live in a spreadsheet?**

The answer is no. Every utility data engineer knows this. The
only question is whether you build governance yourself (diy_total/year)
or use what ships with the platform (uc_ongoing/year).

---

## Part 5: Scaling — What Changes at 1,000 Turbines?

Double the fleet. What happens to governance costs?

### What scales linearly

- **Audit log volume** doubles: more analysts, more queries, more
  events. Storage goes from 3 GB/month to ~6 GB/month.
- **Number of analysts** roughly doubles (30 instead of 15).
- **Data volume** doubles — more tables, more schemas.

scaled_analysts = 30
scaled_audit_gb = 6
scaled_audit_monthly = scaled_audit_gb * $0.023
scaled_audit_annual = scaled_audit_monthly/month over 1 year

### What scales sublinearly

- **Governance engineer time** does NOT double. With attribute-based
  access control (ABAC) — "all analysts in the Texas region can
  see Texas turbine data" — you write policies against attributes,
  not individual users. Adding 15 more analysts to a well-designed
  ABAC model takes minutes, not weeks.

scaled_governance_hours = 5
scaled_governance_weekly = engineer_hourly * scaled_governance_hours
scaled_governance_annual = scaled_governance_weekly/week over 1 year

At 500 turbines, governance costs governance_annual/year. At
1,000 turbines, it costs scaled_governance_annual/year — a 60%
increase for a 100% increase in fleet size. That's the power of
policy-based governance over spreadsheet-based governance.

### What scales superlinearly (the risk)

NERC audit risk goes UP with fleet size. More turbines means more
CEII, more access points, more people, more surface area for
findings. A 1,000-turbine fleet with spreadsheet governance is
not 2x the risk of a 500-turbine fleet — it's substantially worse,
because the complexity of access relationships grows
combinatorially.

scaled_findings = 6
scaled_audit_risk = typical_settlement * scaled_findings

The governance cost barely moves. The risk exposure grows.
This is why every large utility ends up with a governance
platform — the alternative gets worse faster than the solution
gets more expensive.

---

## Detailed Breakdown (computed)

Change any assumption above and rerun to see how these move.

| Category | Annual | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **Unity Catalog governance** | | |
| Engineer time (3 hrs/week) | {{governance_annual}} | Permissions, audit review, access requests |
| Audit log storage (3 GB/month) | {{audit_storage_annual}} | S3 Standard, us-east-1 |
| Training (one-time, year 1) | {{training_onetime}} | 2 days x 3 engineers |
| **Year 1 total** | **{{total_governance_yr1}}** | |
| **Year 2+ total** | **{{total_governance_ongoing}}** | |
| | | |
| **DIY governance** | | |
| Data catalog (Atlan/Alation) | {{diy_catalog_annual}} | ~$3K/month |
| Integration engineering (20% FTE) | {{diy_engineering}} | Glue code, custom audit |
| **DIY total** | **{{diy_total}}** | |
| | | |
| **Risk without governance** | | |
| One NERC finding (typical) | {{typical_settlement}} | $500K–$1M range |
| Likely audit exposure (4 findings) | {{audit_risk}} | First serious audit |
| Manual compliance staff | {{manual_compliance_annual}} | 10 hrs/week, still inadequate |
| | | |
| **At 1,000 turbines** | | |
| UC governance (scaled) | {{scaled_governance_annual}} | 5 hrs/week, ABAC policies |
| Audit risk (scaled) | {{scaled_audit_risk}} | More CEII, more surface area |

### Try it yourself

Set `likely_findings = 1` and rerun. Even with a single finding,
UC pays for itself for over half a century. Set
`governance_hours_weekly = 4` (upper end) and the break-even
barely changes.

Or set `engineer_hourly = $60` for a lower cost-of-living market.
The governance cost drops, but so does the manual compliance
alternative — and the NERC fines don't care where your engineers
live.
